<header class="head">
  <p class="mini">About</p>
  <h1 class="head__title">One person,<br>no sponsors,<br><em>no photographs.</em></h1>
</header>

<section class="band">
  <div class="prose prose--lead">
    <p>
      This is a gazetteer rather than a magazine. Every country, place and stay
      on it has a record, every record says the same things in the same order,
      and the whole thing is meant to be scanned rather than read start to
      finish.
    </p>

    <h2>How the scoring works</h2>
    <p>
      Five points, answering one question: would I tell someone else to go? It
      is not a measure of how nice somewhere is. A place can be spectacular and
      score three because it is crowded, overpriced, or better a hundred miles
      further on.
    </p>

    <h2>Why there are no photographs</h2>
    <p>
      There were, and they were mostly filler. Rather than pad the pages with
      pictures that were not taken where they claim to be, the site now carries
      none at all until there are real ones to publish. Every record is
      structured to take them the moment they exist.
    </p>

    <h2>Placeholders</h2>
    <p>
      Anything tagged <span class="flag">Placeholder</span> is unfinished. The
      writing on those entries is a draft standing in for a real account, and
      they are marked so nobody mistakes them for something I did.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      Static HTML, one small stylesheet, and no JavaScript at all. No web
      fonts, no analytics, no third-party requests of any kind. Every page is
      readable the moment the HTML arrives.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
